# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- scope, caveats, contract map
CAVEATS_INTRO = "Read this chapter before using any number in the book for anything other than learning. The case is a teaching case."
CAVEATS = [
 ("Illustrative teaching case", "The inputs (US$ fares, a 6.5% all-in US$ debt rate, inflation falling from 7% to 3%, a 2.25x target DSCR, a 25.25% required return) are not calibrated to any market. Nothing in this book or the app is investment, legal, tax or credit advice. The credit agreement, the concession and the applicable tax law govern a real transaction; covenant levels quoted are examples."),
 ("One currency", "All amounts are in US$ thousands and revenue is effectively treated as US$ or fully FX-indexed. A Nigerian passenger-rail project would earn naira fares that are often politically set and pay US$ debt: the currency mismatch, indexation lag and convertibility/transfer risk are a lender's first questions. Mitigants to study: local-currency tranches (for example guaranteed local bonds), hedging, political-risk or partial-risk cover (for example MIGA, a partial risk guarantee, ATI), and fare indexation to FX. The model contains none of these. The inflation path (7% to 3%) is a US$ path."),
 ("Tax is jurisdiction-specific", "The model uses a 30% corporate tax rate, tax depreciation of 5% of the written-down value per quarter (about 18.5% a year), full deductibility of senior and shareholder-loan interest, and cash refunds of tax losses. In Nigeria verify the current rules (the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and its commencement): the corporate rate and any development levy or minimum effective tax, capital-allowance rules and restrictions, loss relief (carry-forward, not refund), limits on interest paid to foreign connected persons (a cap by reference to EBITDA with carry-forward of the excess), transfer-pricing rules for the shareholder-loan rate, withholding tax on interest and dividends and treaty relief, and any sector incentives."),
 ("Shareholder loans", "Interest on a shareholder loan is deductible only within interest-limitation rules and at an arm's-length rate; payments are restricted payments under the credit agreement (same lock-up and distribution conditions as dividends); withholding tax applies. A structure with all sponsor funding as loan and no share capital is not permitted by company law or lenders."),
 ("Sizing versus covenants", "Debt is sized once, on the lender's case (for demand-risk rail often a P90 traffic case; in this course the Base case plays that role, and a real lender's own P90 case would normally support less than the 72,154 shown), and the other scenarios then test that fixed structure. The book therefore shows both the re-solved scenarios (useful for structuring) and the Downside and breakeven cases on the FIXED financing (what a credit committee reads). Sizing DSCRs differ from covenant levels: lock-up about 1.10x to 1.20x and default about 1.00x to 1.10x, tested at each calculation date on a historic (and often also a projected) 12-month basis; LLCR tests use the projections."),
 ("What this model leaves out", "Construction contingency, development and advisory fees, initial DSRA funding in the uses, a major-maintenance reserve account (rolling-stock overhauls), hedging, cash sweep, ramp-up, refinancing, an equity bridge, standby equity or a cost-overrun facility, change-in-law and compensation events, lock-up tests on dividends and shareholder-loan payments, a DSRA draw to cover a shortfall, tax-loss carry-forward (app option only), and timing of sponsor funding in the IRR (see R1)."),
 ("Confidentiality when using AI tools", "Do not paste confidential client or transaction data into AI tools that your organisation has not approved. The prompts in this book use only the public teaching case."),
]
